{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/290627-capturing-geist-s-themes-in-an-introduction","id":290627,"title":"Capturing Geist's Themes in an Introduction","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Geist: The Sin-Eaters"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-23T10:16:49-07:00","id":290627,"title":"Capturing Geist's Themes in an Introduction","content":"I wrote and introduction for Geist (located <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1arLby5_aqlyOWrhSyyLsI3LSuF7rt9voAuHSSYdL2HM/edit\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>) for two reasons. First, Geist is a notoriously hard game to wrap one's head around, thematically. Its hard to picture what you're supposed to <i>do</i> in a Geist game and I feel a well written introduction would assist people with that.<br /> <br /> The second reason is my attempts to improve as a writer. Blood and Smoke remains one of the very few books I've read from cover to cover, straight through, and enjoyed basically every moment of. It's a fantastic presentation of Vampire, regardless of how you feel about individual choices. I am attempting to emulate the style and decisions in another setting (in this case Geist) and examining how it works with a new setting and set of themes. Where it doesn't work, I'll examine why and perhaps what works in its stead.<br /> <br /> So, if your first thought is like, &quot;Isn't this just the Introduction to Blood and Smoke with the serial numbers filed off?&quot; the answer is yes. It is, and it's supposed to be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1157,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","user":"SoulGambit","date":". 10-23-2014, 02:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-23T12:37:04-07:00","id":290719,"title":"","content":"That's a pretty good introduction, and it nicely phrases a lot of the key themes. I think there are a few more that deserved to be touched upon, however:<br /> <br /> To me, the most important theme is the resolution of <i>peoples'</i> lives. The whole game is filled with human motivations and stories, down to the momentos which bear witness to meaningful death. I love geist for its ability to highlight, with a good storyteller, the very best and very worst in humanity - and all of it is cut short before it plays out the way it needs to; even the Bounds' own lives. A lot of this you touch on, I just... feel like that should be ribbon around the cover - the obvious, upfront binding theme of the game which is put forth clearly and strongly. That might just be me though....<br /> <br /> Also:<br /> the constant companionship with a monster and the relationship you form with it [personally, I get really annoyed with calls to make your geist the primary antagonist and beef its mechanics up to the point of body-jacking the PCs, but even as it currently stands its a powerful narrative force, and several antagonist groups are emblematic of falling into the wrong kind of relationship with your second life partner];<br /> clean and necessary death vs acts of monstrosity [GtSE touches on themes that death is necessary, and the PCs, whether they should or not, often choose who to save and who to hurry along -<br /> in addition to which ghosts to help, ignore, exploit, or destroy. Planned murder isn't a Synergy breaking point, for instance, while abusive/torturous/ritualistic murders create ghosts which are the hardest to help and the most dangerous. It's a theme neatly expressed by the Reaper vs Abmortal dynamic.]<br /> <br /> And I feel like there's a theme that Krewe Binding brings out, but I'm not able to nail down exactly what. It's probably part of the other key themes - people's lives and the allure of the underworld, certainly. I've always like the bit where what you believe is true is revealed - especially if you don't want to believe it, especially if its terrible. It really does focus the game so centrally on the struggles of the characters, as everything is supposed to conform to those revelations; Sin Eaters don't doubt their revelations, because everything they learn about death reinforces it. I know it's an odd part of the game a lot of people are turned off by (and, like most of the book, it really deserves to be expanded and cleaned up a lot more), but it does bring out a certain.... thing.<br /> <br /> <br /> Those are just my musings about the themes of <i>Geist</i>. I certainly agree with everything else you wrote up, and I think you introduced them quite well.<br /> I think it's very helpful to narrow in on what Geist is &quot;supposed to&quot; be about, what and why people enjoy it, as part of the conversation about (hopefully) 2E.<br /> Nice work.<br /> <br /> ~Seraph Kitty","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-23T14:26:33-07:00","id":290796,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seraph Kitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/290627-capturing-geist-s-themes-in-an-introduction?p=290719#post290719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I feel like there's a theme that Krewe Binding brings out, but I'm not able to nail down exactly what. It's probably part of the other key themes - people's lives and the allure of the underworld, certainly. I've always like the bit where what you believe is true is revealed - especially if you don't want to believe it, especially if its terrible. It really does focus the game so centrally on the struggles of the characters, as everything is supposed to conform to those revelations; Sin Eaters don't doubt their revelations, because everything they learn about death reinforces it. I know it's an odd part of the game a lot of people are turned off by (and, like most of the book, it really deserves to be expanded and cleaned up a lot more), but it does bring out a certain.... thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kicking together the common citation of latter-day Persona and the previewed Mastigos writeup for Mage 2e (particularly given the shared bit of Jung that I kept hammering at when I wrote my term paper on Geist), I think the notion behind the confrontation with the shadow is salient here - these things and thoughts are greatly horrifying because they have great power in your psyche, and failing to acknowledge them (whether you like them or not) gives rise to pain and disaster.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-23T15:53:42-07:00","id":290832,"title":"","content":"It looks very good good to me.<br /> <br /> Two things coming from there:<br /> <br /> <b>Geist</b> is the Storytelling Game of Second Chances. You have a second chance to change your life and how you interact with the world. What do you do with it? Outside of the whole ghosts thing, how are you living different? Are you a different person than before? People with near death experiences often talk about what they will change now. You certainly qualify.<br /> <br /> Resolution is a second one, and Seraph Kitty touched on that.Resolutions in other people's life, and yours.<br /> <br /> <b>Geist</b> is a celebratory game, in the sense a wake in celebratory. There is an undercurrent of sadness and morbidity, and those are part of the game. So something upbeat is thematic too, with a hint of melancholy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/